// Histogram — array-output reduction with data-dependent indexing.
//
// Input is a stream of N i32 values, each constrained to the bin
// range [0, BINS). The output is a BINS-element i32 array where
// `histogram[bin]` is the count of input elements that equal `bin`.
// This is PRD §9 row 8 ('reduction with shared output array'),
// distinct from example 03 (reduction-to-scalar) by reducing to an
// array via index-dependent accumulation.
//
// What this example demonstrates:
//   - Array-output accumulator pattern: `histogram[b]` is read and
//     written in the same dataflow statement, indexed by the inner
//     loop variable `b`. Single-assignment (PRD §6.2.1) is on the
//     SYMBOL `histogram`, not on individual indices. The codegen
//     pre-initialises `histogram` to zero (the additive identity).
//   - Data-dependent indexing handled at the KERNEL level, not at
//     the algorithm level. The algorithm uses a rectangular
//     accumulator nest `for i, for b` and the kernel
//     `bin_inc(acc, value, bin)` is a masked increment: returns
//     `acc + 1` iff `value == bin`, else `acc`. This is the same
//     shape as example 04's `exclusive_add(acc, x, b, c)` (which
//     adds `x` iff `c < b`) and `block_scan(acc, x, boff, i, j)`
//     (which adds `x` iff `j <= i`). The pattern was probed and
//     pinned in 04-prefix-sum cycle (TASK-0039): conditional /
//     data-dependent expressions live in kernel bodies (arbitrary
//     Rust the host toolchain compiles, PRD §6.2.2), NOT in the
//     algorithm language (which has no conditionals — PRD §6.2.4).
//   - Array output flowing through `save_output`. Same shape as
//     example 04-prefix-sum's `save_output : (i32[NB][BS]) -> ()`,
//     pinning the aggregate-typed save-kernel surface.
//
// What this example does NOT demonstrate:
//   - Bin indexing with floating-point / non-trivial binning. The
//     input fixture is pre-clipped to [0, BINS-1]; the kernel
//     `bin_inc` does an integer equality check, no division /
//     modulo / range bucketing. A real-world histogram on
//     unconstrained input would either pre-process the value into
//     a bin index in a separate kernel, or do the bucketing inside
//     `bin_inc` directly — both are kernel-level extensions, NOT
//     algorithm-level changes.
//   - Distributed schedule with per-worker partial histograms +
//     cross-worker partial-combine. AC#3 of TASK-0044.04 carries
//     that stretch; cycle 186 shipped the language-sanity slice
//     bit-identical across ALL FOUR tier-1 backends (pthreads-sync,
//     mp-tcp-bufsync, pthreads-async, mp-tcp-event) — exceeding
//     AC#2's pthreads-sync + mp-tcp-bufsync minimum. The
//     distributed schedule lives at `distributed.sched.nuc`; it
//     lowers + emits + runs on all 4 tier-1 backends, but the
//     host-side cross-worker combine emits last-write-wins
//     overwrites instead of element-wise sum. Filed as TASK-0343
//     and `[[skip]]`'d across all 4 backends. See README.
//   - Float reductions. PRD §10.1 demands bit-identity; only
//     integer counts here.
//
// Numeric choice: i32 throughout. Even though the count never goes
// negative for the committed fixture, i32 (not u32) keeps the
// algorithm sublanguage in the same scalar type family as examples
// 03 / 04 — the scalar-only contract pass (TASK-0012) accepts i32
// kernels without aggregate-matching gaps. `wrapping_add` in the
// kernel documents overflow intent; the committed fixture has
// max-bin-count well below i32::MAX.

const N    : usize = 256;     // total input length
const BINS : usize = 16;      // histogram bin count

// Input array and the BINS-wide accumulator output. Each `data`
// symbol is assigned by exactly one dataflow statement
// (PRD §6.2.1).
data input     : i32[N];
data histogram : i32[BINS];

// I/O kernels — effectful, ordering preserved, never duplicated.
kernel load_input  : ()          -> i32[N]    effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[BINS]) -> ()        effectful;

// Per-element bin-increment kernel — pure, reorderable. Returns
// `acc + 1` iff `value == bin`, else `acc`. The conditional /
// data-dependent indexing lives in the Rust kernel body (PRD
// §6.2.2: arbitrary Rust); the algorithm uses only the rectangular
// masked-accumulate shape that examples 03 / 04 already prove
// bit-identical at M1.
kernel bin_inc     : (i32, i32, i32) -> i32   pure;

// Load the entire input on whoever owns I/O (the schedule decides).
input <-- load_input();

// Per-input rectangular masked accumulator. `histogram` is
// pre-initialised to zero by the codegen (additive identity); each
// (i, b) iteration calls `bin_inc(histogram[b], input[i], b)` which
// returns `histogram[b] + 1` iff `input[i] == b`, else
// `histogram[b]`. The fold builds the full histogram across all N
// inputs and all BINS bins.
for i : 0 .. N {
    for b : 0 .. BINS {
        histogram[b] <-- bin_inc(histogram[b], input[i], b);
    }
}

save_output(histogram);
